From: Pyra kernel work <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [EXPERIMENT, NOT A FIX] ASoC: ti: mcpdm: pop six downlink slots for the ABE

NOT APPLIED. Kept because of what it proves and what it exposes.

MCPDM_LINK_DL1 | MCPDM_LINK_DL2 is four downlink slots. The ABE's PDM_DL
port is SIX_MSB: the DSP writes six samples per frame into the downlink
FIFO. With four slots the link pops 4 * 96000 samples/s against the
6 * 96000 the ABE produces, and the reader is lapped.

Measured at the headphone jack into a PC line input, 1 kHz tone:

  slots   modulation      first image (1666.7 Hz)
  4       2666.67 Hz      -5.5 dB      (direct McPDM link: -90.9 dB)
  5       2000.00 Hz      -50.3 dB     (forced by poking CTRL mid-stream)
  6       -- none --      -45.6 dB     (this patch)

The hold rate follows 8000 / (slots - 1) and fits to under 1 Hz, which is
what identifies the slot count as the mechanism. At six slots the rates
match exactly and the imaging is gone: 40 dB of improvement on the first
image.

It is not a fix because it trades that for something worse. With six
slots the output grows harmonics of the tone at full level -- 2f at
-0.5 dB, 3f at -2.8 dB, and the loudest line in the output at 14 kHz --
where four slots had 2f at -58.5 dB. Overall junk-to-tone goes from
-2.5 dB to +12.6 dB, so it sounds worse, not better.

That second artefact is harmonic rather than an image, so it is not a
rate mismatch; it looks like the headset slot receiving the wrong
sample, i.e. frame alignment at link start. omap_mcpdm_start() resets
the downlink, sets the slot mask and releases the reset, with no regard
for where the ABE's port is in its own frame -- the same class of
problem as the start-order bug in 0025.

Whoever picks this up: the slot count is established, the alignment is
the open question.
---
--- a/sound/soc/ti/omap-mcpdm.c
+++ b/sound/soc/ti/omap-mcpdm.c
@@ -57,6 +57,13 @@
 #define MCPDM_LINK_DL1		(0x3 << 3)
 #define MCPDM_LINK_DL2		(0x3 << 5)
 #define MCPDM_LINK_UL1		(0x3 << 0)
+/*
+ * The two downlink slots past DL2. Nothing is wired to them, but the ABE's
+ * PDM_DL port writes SIX_MSB -- six samples per frame -- and the link has to
+ * pop all six or the frame rates do not match. See the comment in
+ * omap_mcpdm_dai_hw_params().
+ */
+#define MCPDM_LINK_DL_PAD	(0x3 << 7)
 
 struct mcpdm_link_config {
 	u32 link_mask; /* channel mask for the direction */
@@ -392,11 +399,34 @@
 		u32 mask;
 
 		switch (dai->id) {
+		/*
+		 * Four downlink slots is what DL1 and DL2 add up to, and it is
+		 * the wrong number. The ABE's PDM_DL port is SIX_MSB: the DSP
+		 * writes six samples per frame into the downlink FIFO and the
+		 * ATC hands them to this link. With four slots enabled the
+		 * link pops four per frame against the six being written, so
+		 * it consumes 4 * 96000 where the ABE produces 6 * 96000, and
+		 * the reader is lapped.
+		 *
+		 * What that sounds like, measured at the headphone jack with
+		 * a 1 kHz tone: the output is a zero-order hold at 8000 / 3 =
+		 * 2666.67 Hz, with images at every n * 2666.67 +/- 1000 Hz.
+		 * The first of them, at 1666.7 Hz, comes out 6 dB below the
+		 * tone -- the direct McPDM link puts it at -85 dB -- and a
+		 * 5 kHz tone folds down to an alias at 2333 Hz louder than
+		 * the tone itself. The hold rate follows the slot count as
+		 * 8000 / (slots - 1), confirmed by forcing five slots and
+		 * watching it move to 2000 Hz exactly.
+		 *
+		 * So pad the downlink mask out to six. The two extra slots
+		 * carry the zeros the DSP already writes in SIX_MSB's unused
+		 * channels, and the rates match: 6 * 96000 either side.
+		 */
 		case OMAP_MCPDM_DAI_ABE_DL1:
-			mask = MCPDM_LINK_DL1;
+			mask = MCPDM_LINK_DL1 | MCPDM_LINK_DL_PAD;
 			break;
 		case OMAP_MCPDM_DAI_ABE_DL2:
-			mask = MCPDM_LINK_DL2;
+			mask = MCPDM_LINK_DL2 | MCPDM_LINK_DL_PAD;
 			break;
 		default:
 			mask = MCPDM_LINK_UL1;
